<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
        This project belongs to no solution, on purpose. It looks like an oversight and is not, so the
        reasoning lives here rather than in anyone's memory.

        What it demonstrates is the consumer that sits OUTSIDE the solution it checks. The adapter
        finds its target by walking up from the test assembly for a named solution file, which is the
        shape a team gets when its architecture tests live beside the code instead of inside the
        solution under test. That walk is the whole point of the project, and its sixteen lines are
        the whole demonstration; declaring it as a member dissolves the thing being shown.

        Joining a solution would also break two working guarantees.

        Spec discovery. The CLI picks a spec project by convention: the unique DECLARED solution
        member that references the LoadBearing contract library. This project references the xUnit
        adapter, which references that library, so as a declared member it becomes a second candidate
        and the check verb stops with "Multiple spec projects found" and a non-zero exit. That would
        fail the examples job, the packaged quickstart walk, and the published quickstart itself,
        whose entire point is that it runs without being told where the spec lives.

        The committed drawing. Its survey fence draws every project its solution declares, and a gate
        holds it to exactly that set, while a second gate requires the LoadBearing projects to appear
        nowhere in the body. A declared test project would demand a redrawn survey with a test node
        in it, drawn from a spec that is not what the page is about.

        None of this leaves the project uncovered. The examples workflow builds and runs it on every
        push, in a step named for this adapter, which deliberately does not reuse an earlier build
        because no solution above it produces one. Its nine rule tests run there.

        What it genuinely lacks is solution-scoped static inspection, which cannot reach a project
        that no solution declares. That is accepted rather than overlooked:
        one file, one class, one overridden property.
    -->

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
        <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
        <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
        <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- Microsoft.Testing.Platform, not VSTest: xunit.v3 4.0.0 ships MTP 2.x, which the .NET 10 SDK
         declines to drive through the VSTest target. Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk and xunit.runner.visualstudio
         were the VSTest half and are gone. What a quickstart reader copies is this ItemGroup, so it is the
         shape a consumer of the adapter needs: the metapackage alone. -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" Version="4.0.0" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\..\src\Zphil.LoadBearing.Xunit\Zphil.LoadBearing.Xunit.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\arch\Meridian.Quoting.ArchSpec\Meridian.Quoting.ArchSpec.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
